## Foundation (1505–1515)
### Afonso de Albuquerque
To enforce his power over the growing empire's population, the great majority of which was not Portuguese, Albuquerque resorted to medieval Iberian procedures: people of different religions were allowed to live by their own laws under representatives of their respective communities.[[24]](https://www.google.com/goto?url=CAESaQHrOzAV7yoo5tK4c9PAnIOVWdf6IwmrfIh-GMXjnqV1KEetG51icmgXRIMxmi9OkLveb49MvFtSjuhckl1WPxiPHMKWcS7jTvrRd5eC5S6ZzgW9oGjQaEqLTwPyq3lYl06NNBUU9jrQYg#cite_note-24) An exception was made for the practice of sati, which was abolished, along with certain taxes due to the Sultan of Bijapur.[[25]](https://www.google.com/goto?url=CAESaQHrOzAV7yoo5tK4c9PAnIOVWdf6IwmrfIh-GMXjnqV1KEetG51icmgXRIMxmi9OkLveb49MvFtSjuhckl1WPxiPHMKWcS7jTvrRd5eC5S6ZzgW9oGjQaEqLTwPyq3lYl06NNBUU9jrQYg#cite_note-25) Albuquerque encouraged the settlement of his soldiers and their marriage to native women, offering financial incentives for those who did;

